The Melvil Decimal System (MDS) is a classification system designed 
to organize materials by subjects, enabling easier access and 
efficient cataloging.

Overview
The Melvil Decimal System was created by Melvil Dewey in 1876 and is more 
commonly known today as the Dewey Decimal Classification (DDC), though some 
references might use "Melvil Decimal System" to emphasize its founder’s name. 
The system is widely used in libraries around the world to categorize and 
arrange materials according to subject matter.

Structure
MDS organizes knowledge into 10 main classes, each represented by a three-
digit number ranging from 000 to 900:

000 – General works, computer science, information
100 – Philosophy and psychology
200 – Religion
300 – Social sciences
400 – Language
500 – Natural sciences and mathematics
600 – Technology (applied sciences)
700 – Arts and recreation
800 – Literature
900 – History and geography

Each main class is further subdivided into 10 divisions, which themselves 
can be broken down into 10 sections, allowing for detailed categorization. 
For example, 510 represents Mathematics (500 is Natural sciences), and 516 
represents Geometry.

Principles and Features
Decimal notation: The use of decimals allows for hierarchical and limitless 
subdivision of subjects.

Flexibility: New topics can be integrated without major restructuring.

Universal applicability: Designed for general knowledge, making it suitable 
for libraries of all sizes.

Ease of use: Numeric codes simplify shelving and retrieval compared to 
purely alphabetical arrangements.

Application
In practice, libraries label books with MDS numbers on the spine to group 
similar subjects together, helping both librarians and patrons locate 
materials quickly. Many modern libraries also integrate the system into 
digital cataloging software, linking MDS numbers with metadata and search 
functions.

Historical Significance

The MDS, or Dewey Decimal System, revolutionized library management by 
providing a standardized, systematic classification. While alternative 
systems like the Library of Congress Classification exist, MDS remains 
popular worldwide due to its simplicity and scalability. Its adoption 
facilitated the growth of public, academic, and school libraries in the 
20th century.

In summary, the Melvil Decimal System is a structured, hierarchical 
method for organizing library materials by subject, making it easier 
to access, manage, and expand collections efficiently.
